<ProxyEndpoint name="default">
  <HTTPProxyConnection>
    <BasePath>/api</BasePath>
  </HTTPProxyConnection>

  <FaultRules>
    <FaultRule name="rate-limit-to-429">
      <Step><Name>AM-RateLimit429</Name></Step>
      <Condition>(fault.name = "SpikeArrestViolation") or (fault.name = "QuotaViolation")</Condition>
    </FaultRule>
  </FaultRules>

  <PreFlow name="PreFlow">
    <Request>
      <Step><Name>SA-SpikeArrest</Name></Step>
      <Step><Name>VJ-EntraToken</Name></Step>
      <Step><Name>AM-UserClaims</Name></Step>
      <Step><Name>AM-QuotaKeys</Name></Step>
      <!--
        jwt_roles !contains "..." fails live deployment:
        "expressions.parser.OperandsShouldBeLogical: Both the operands for
        NOT expression should be logical" — confirmed 2026-09-07, exactly
        the risk docs/20's own §5.4 note flagged before ever deploying this.
        Using the documented fallback: NOT (... Matches "*...*"), since
        Matches already returns a logical boolean and jwt_roles is already
        a plain string (AM-UserClaims.xml assigns it from the claim),
        so no separate flattened variable is needed.
      -->
      <Step>
        <Name>RF-TranslationRoleRequired</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/translation/**" AND NOT (jwt_roles Matches "*Translation.User*")</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>RF-SalesRoleRequired</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/sales/**" AND NOT (jwt_roles Matches "*SalesAgent.User*")</Condition>
      </Step>
      <!--
        NaaS.User gates the whole /naas/** prefix and nothing finer. The
        admin endpoints (/naas/v1/admin/tickets/**) are gated by the
        APPLICATION, from its ADMIN_ROLES setting, against the roles this
        proxy forwards in x-colt-user-roles — there is deliberately no
        RF-NaaSAdminRoleRequired step here. Adding one would put the same
        authorization decision in two places that can disagree.
      -->
      <Step>
        <Name>RF-NaaSRoleRequired</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/naas/**" AND NOT (jwt_roles Matches "*NaaS.User*")</Condition>
      </Step>
      <!--
        The one place a second, finer role is enforced on a sub-path. Ordinary
        NaaS endpoints need only NaaS.User above; the admin sub-path also needs
        NaaS.Admin, because POST /naas/v1/admin/tickets/{id}/approve approves a
        real, irreversible Ethernet Port order.

        This duplicates a decision the application also makes (its ADMIN_ROLES
        setting) — accepted deliberately for this endpoint only. The two must
        change together; see RF-NaaSAdminRoleRequired.xml for the full
        reasoning and why Platform.Admin is excluded.

        Note the suffix starts at /naas/v1/... because BasePath /api is
        stripped before pathsuffix is evaluated.
      -->
      <Step>
        <Name>RF-NaaSAdminRoleRequired</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/naas/v1/admin/**" AND NOT (jwt_roles Matches "*NaaS.Admin*")</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step><Name>VA-ApiKey</Name></Step>
      <!--
        Q-PerMinute stays a flat, platform-wide, per-user RATE guard (abuse
        protection, not a budget) — unchanged, still shared across all three
        use cases, same role as SA-SpikeArrest one layer further up.

        The daily BUDGET hierarchy below it replaces the old flat Q-PerDay.
        Three tiers, nested: quota(use case) x quota(department within it) x
        quota(user within that department). Only the step matching the
        request's own path fires, exactly like RF-*RoleRequired above —
        that Step Condition IS the use-case separation; nothing in Tier 2 or
        Tier 3's Identifier mentions the use case at all.

        THIS RAISES THE PLATFORM'S TOTAL POSSIBLE DAILY REQUEST VOLUME. The
        old Q-PerDay was one shared 5,000/day ceiling across all three apps
        together; the new Tier-1 ceilings are per use case and sum higher
        than that. Deliberate — see aicoe-standard's product description and
        BUILD-LOG.md for the sizing record — not a side effect to discover
        later.
      -->
      <Step><Name>Q-PerMinute</Name></Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Translation-UsecaseDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/translation/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Translation-DeptDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/translation/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Translation-DeptUserDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/translation/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Sales-UsecaseDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/sales/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Sales-DeptDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/sales/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Sales-DeptUserDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/sales/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Naas-UsecaseDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/naas/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Naas-DeptDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/naas/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>Q-Naas-DeptUserDay</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/naas/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <!--
        Which backend's Google ID token audience to mint is now a runtime
        KVM lookup, not a build-time choice of TargetEndpoint — both paths
        route through the SAME "backends" Target Server (PSC, see
        targets/backends.xml); the internal load balancer's own URL map
        (terraform/6-workloads/6c-gclt-aicoe-dev-ingress/main.tf) is what
        actually dispatches /translation/* vs /sales/* to the right Cloud
        Run service. Apigee's only remaining job is picking the right
        audience for the ID token it mints for that hop.
      -->
      <Step>
        <Name>KVM-GetTranslationAudience</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/translation/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>KVM-GetSalesAudience</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/sales/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step>
        <Name>KVM-GetNaasAudience</Name>
        <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/naas/**"</Condition>
      </Step>
      <Step><Name>AM-StripAndInjectHeaders</Name></Step>
    </Request>
    <Response/>
  </PreFlow>

  <RouteRule name="backends">
    <Condition>proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/translation/**" OR proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/sales/**" OR proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/naas/**"</Condition>
    <TargetEndpoint>backends</TargetEndpoint>
  </RouteRule>
  <RouteRule name="no-match">
    <!-- no condition = default. If neither path matched above, this returns nothing -> 404. -->
  </RouteRule>
</ProxyEndpoint>
